#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084102} :activity/id #uuid "ec6c85e9-6830-57b2-8009-2f8599700364" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048135 :topic/id #uuid "8257a26c-85bf-580e-9d81-e2194473d981" :topic/math-academy-id 793} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084102 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084442 :tutorial/content "Suppose we roll a fair die. Then, the sample space $\\mathcal S$ of this experiment is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{S} = \\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the event\n$X = \\{6\\}$.\nThis means that $X$ is the event \"the die lands on $6$.\" We know that\n\n$$\nP(X) = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nbecause there are $6$ equally likely outcomes and only $1$ of those outcomes is a $6$.\n\nWe can represent the entire sample space $\\mathcal S$, the event $X$, and its probability using a **Venn diagram**.\n\n![](images/96/966be6be009751b755fbcaca9f037e0c4ee281525a94e50a9a19631dc7cd4e1f.png)\n\nNote the following important points:\n\n- The area inside the rectangle represents the sample space $\\mathcal S$.\n- The area inside the circle (shaded blue) represents the event $X$.\n- The number inside the blue circle represents the probability of $X$.\n- The area inside the rectangle but outside the circle represents the event $X'$, the complement of $X$. In our case, the complement is\n$X' = \\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$,\nwhich consists of every event other than throwing a $6$.\n\nFor any event $X$, we have\n\n$$\nP(X) +P\\left(X'\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nNote that the above formula can be rearranged into either of the following equivalent forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X) &= 1 - P(X^{′}) \\\\\nP(X^{′}) &= 1 - P(X)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we can calculate\n\n$$\nP\\left(X'\\right)\n$$\n\nas\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X^{′}) &= 1 - P(X) \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{1}{6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the sample space $\\mathcal S$ contains every possible event, we sometimes write\n\n$$\nP(\\mathcal S) = 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "af5371a2-dcfe-5de0-b0ab-f82c96a0b759" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3657 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "93f32069-023f-5d69-983e-2273e36c3952" :step/math-academy-id 20492 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084132}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083212 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083011 :question/id #uuid "c9db939d-eb4f-5516-b95d-666e235a95c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5621"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fbb5348e-e292-513f-8b0a-90428692fc0a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049752}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234455}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Probability of the Complement Given the Probability of the Event"} :step/id #uuid "87f1844e-77bf-5d6f-a5cc-5bf66a479b5f" :step/math-academy-id 20495 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084104}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084104 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083213 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083012 :question/id #uuid "fc8c6d6b-1596-5732-afe0-783b176339be" :question/math-academy-id "e-5624"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af4cfa4a-7f49-50c9-a875-ce51c3874c4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234042}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Probability of an Event Given the Probability of the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "113d7621-4637-5ddb-bcfe-a8f99fc1bb72" :step/math-academy-id 20502 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084105}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083214 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083014 :question/id #uuid "a761cb7f-122c-5ca0-8e90-109e0df60f3b" :question/math-academ